across the continent extreme weather  caused by climate changes upending  people's lives forcing them to flee  their homes and pushing Millions into  hunger and starvation there are Grim new  figures from the World Meteorological  organization they say Africa is warming  faster than any other place in the world  and with that agricultural productivity  is declining the rise in sea levels is  increasing along the African Coastline  at a higher rate than the global average  threatening low-lying coastal cities and  extreme weather events like floods and  droughts are increasing in frequency and  intensity killing thousands of people  large swathes of Africa are already  experiencing acute drought and one of  the worst affected countries right now  is Madagascar where the rains have  failed for years our correspondent  Adrian Krish visited the above region  massive watsaga used to be a farmer now  all she is able to harvest our cactus  leaves a rentless drought has left large  parts of Southern Madagascar dry and  unproductive and it has left people like  Mercy in a precarious situation  she now relies on turning cactus leaves  into cattle feet to survive since the  rains stopped a life has been turned on  its head  we left the village because of the  drought it's very difficult to find  water there there was no Harvest so we  had to sell all our belongings  many others are suffering the same fate  as Mercy she lives alongside them in  this informal camp in the town of Ambu  vombe Trader damiheri salal opened the  camp when he saw how many people had  nowhere to go after being forced to  leave their Villages  this old man lying here he used to have  a 120 head of cattle he was a rich man  but over the years he had to sell  everything he owned  and when the people have nothing to eat  they end up just lying here like this  [Music]  here in the region they call it the  hunger people got used to not having  enough food going to bed hungry it is  incredible how they managed to adapt to  this hostile environment but of course  it comes at a price  in charge of the malnutrition unit for  children at the hospital in Abu Bombay  she says many children here are  chronically ill last month two children  came too late and died  this little girl is one of the five she  is currently treating  this baby is 17 months old  her arm circumference is very low it's  in the red part  98 millimeters  normally it should be 130 for her age  she still can't stand  normally at this age children already  run she can't even stand up  no  we don't have good food at home  sometimes we mix the nutrition Aid that  ngos give us with cassava  and we cook the cassava with a lot of  water if I have some money I buy sweet  potatoes  back at the camp damihari salal says he  tries to help here and there by buying  medicine and collecting donations he  believes mainly climate change is to  blame for the situation but he also says  corruption by NGO staff and government  incompetence are making matters worse  for decades the government fails to  invest in the region the cycle of  poverty gets worse as rainfall gets less  it is definitely going to be very  difficult to stop this crisis  unless the aid organizations clean up  their Acts  and it will also be difficult if the  government doesn't see what's really  happening on the ground to take the  right measures  we need a Hands-On approach to bring  this situation to an end  Erman  as she prepares the first and only meal  of the day for her family massive wat  Saga asks herself why this is happening  I don't know why the rain doesn't fall  it may be because of because of humans  we don't love each other maybe that's  why the rain has stopped falling or  because strangers did something to  prevent the rain I don't know  some greens and two cups of rice that's  all the family of 14 can afford for  today yet they still offer their  neighbors to join trying to help  Madagascar and to talk more about this I  am joined by Mohammed abdika he is  director for East Africa and the Horn of  Africa at the un's International  Organization for migration it's good to  have you on the program thank you so  much for being here Mr aptica let's  begin by talking about the migration in  Africa that is being caused by climate  change just what is the scale of this  challenge thank you for having me here  today first we need to talk about  climate change we can no longer ignore  the issue of displacement mobility and  migration and we never used to talk  about that and right now we bring that  issue of mobility in migration that is  becoming key for us a good example is in  Somalia where today we have over 1.3  million people displaced because of slow  induced climate migration 1.3 million  people at the same time you have close  to 7.8 million people in need of food  assistance and if we don't act very  quickly we have over 288  thousand people who are an acute food  insecurity who might die because of lack  of food that just gives you an example  of the scale in Somalia but if you look  at the region itself over 36.1 million  people in little assistance because of  slow onset climate emerges that we're  dealing with today  well I mean these these numbers are  astonishing and you talked about action  and when I get to that point um in a  short moment but let's talk about the  patterns if we can when people do move  how do they move it and where are they  likely to go I think right now what's  happened you see a lot of rural urban  migration I can use Somali as a case  study where we're seeing a large number  of somalis moving away from rural areas  to the capital city or to the cities  within the country itself if you talk  about baido at the Southwest stage you  have hundreds of thousands of somalis  who've also run away from al-shabab held  areas it's an extremist group they run  from there now they're going to buy  though are looking for assistance and we  have over 10 000 for example arrived  last week alone 10 000 people asking for  assistance whether you when and  humanitarian partners are providing food  shelter Health non-food items for them  people to deal with this but that is  just in Somalia you have some of them in  Burundi you have the rising Waters of  Lake Tanganyika which is displacing  hundreds of boron deals themselves you  go to South Sudan there's a whole city  called Bento underwater the last seven  months because of climate change  Mr abdica um here in Europe uh the focus  is on the migrants who who take the  route uh of crossing the Mediterranean  many have died doing so but in Eastern  Africa where you work there are also  dangerous routes that people are taking  can you tell us more about that um the  biggest road the biggest Maritime route  in the world is called the Eastern Road  the biggest Maritime route in the world  everybody talks about Libya  Mediterranean to Europe that's right  Beverly talks about Mexico U.S border  but when you talk about the Eastern  Europe you talk about the largest  Maritime migration route in the world  from the Horn of Africa through Somalia  Djibouti the Red Sea to Yemen which is a  country in Conflict to Saudi Arabia  every year we have a close to 150 000  people 150 000 people who cross through  these countries in deserts been smuggled  and trafficked going into Yemen conflict  Zone and going into Saudi Arabia at the  same time we have Saudi Arabia deporting  a large number of these migrants back to  home countries a good example is in  Ethiopia they've deported the last seven  months a hundred and three thousand  Ethiopians back to Ethiopia itself  um a lot of those people on the Move  would classify as being climate change  refugees people who've been displaced  because of climate change  um generally is this issue being given  enough attention that is is the  International Community committing  enough money to helping people in this  desperate position you've you've told us  some really astonishing numbers the what  we've come up with what we call the  migrant response plan for Horn of Africa  and Yemen it's appealing for about close  to 100 million dollars to support these  migrants in Djibouti in Yemen  in Somalia in Ethiopia itself we are  getting some support not as much as we  wanted to get but it's not as much  support as we would like to see and we  always appeal to our development  partners and donors to increase that  support we've seen the smuggling the  trafficking we've seen the economic  impact this has on these migrants and  we're seeing a very different Trend  which is a feminization of migration  before it used to be men on the move  trying to feed their families but now  we're seeing more and more women and  girls on the move as well and that is  what is worrying us more because of the  trafficking the abuse the gender-based  violence they go through on the end  route to their migration those trying to  reach mostly Saudi Arabia that's where  they want to go that's where we have the  domestic workers it's irregular migrants  themselves and that has an impact but  also it's not just climate induced  migration what we have is very much an  economic reason we ask them all the time  why are you on the move and all of them  tell us I have no job it's poverty it's  a lack of economic opportunities in the  region and people on the Move well Mr  abdica we could really go on for a lot  longer but I think we we can leave it  here right now that is Mohammed abdica  from the international organization of  migration thank you sir thank you thank  you for having me
 

